Reactions on the detail page now show what was actually saved. If a save fails, the user gets an error and the reaction goes back to its stored state. The existing tests pass (106 files, 1427 tests), and I didn't edit any tests. I didn't add a test for this, and I haven't checked it in a running app.

**What was wrong** (in `packages/app-shell/src/views/RecordDetailView.tsx`, `handleToggleReaction`):
- **Failures were hidden.** The save's error was caught and ignored, so the reaction stayed on screen with no message.
- **Double saves.** The save request was sent from inside a React state update. React can run that code twice (in development, StrictMode does this), so one click could save twice.

**How it works now:**
- **Every click counts.** Each comment keeps its last saved reactions plus a queue of clicks that haven't been saved yet. Clicks made while an earlier save is still running go into the queue, and the screen shows all of them right away.
- **One save at a time.** Saves run in click order. Each one starts from the last saved state, so a later save never re-sends a change that failed earlier.
- **Success:** the saved state moves forward.
- **Failure:** that click is dropped from the queue, the reaction goes back to what's stored (plus any clicks still waiting), and an error toast appears.
- **Each save is sent once**, from outside the state update.
- **No data connection:** clicking a reaction now shows the error instead of a change that looks saved but isn't.

I added a `detail.reactionFailed` error message to all 10 locale files, next to the existing `commentFailed` message, so the check that every language has the same keys still passes.

**Unchanged limitations:**
- The format sent to the server is the same as before. It doesn't know who else reacted, so other people are saved as placeholders.
- If the comments reload while a save is still running, the reload can overwrite the reactions on screen until the queue next updates them.

The type check found no errors in the new code. Its only errors were about workspace packages that aren't built locally.
